## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical application of cache-aside in CNPA, beginner-friendly yet concrete, focusing on real-world latency under load.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern
- TTL tuning and backoff
- Cache stampede protection (distributed locks)
- Observability: hit rate, latency, QPS
- Failure modes and fallback

## Code Example
```javascript
async function getUser(id){
  const cacheKey = `user:${id}`;
  let v = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (v) return JSON.parse(v);
  const lockKey = `lock:${cacheKey}`;
  const acquired = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', { NX: true, PX: 2000 });
  if (!acquired){
    await sleep(50);
    return getUser(id);
  }
  const row = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=$1', [id]);
  await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(row), 'EX', 300);
  await redis.del(lockKey);
  return row;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Redis outage or cache miss storms when Redis is unavailable?
- How would you validate improvements with load testing and chaos scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about end-to-end exactly-once semantics across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in CNPA stacks, including failure modes and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once across services
- Outbox pattern and transactional boundaries
- Deduplication using client_request_id
- Idempotent sinks/consumers and cache invalidation
- Observability: duplicates, latency, retry rates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: HTTP handler scaffold
async function handleOrder(req, res) {
  const id = req.headers['client_request_id'];
  if (!id) return res.status(400).send('missing id');
  await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO orders (...) VALUES (...);');
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO dedupe (client_request_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;', [id]);
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event) VALUES (...);');
  });
  res.status(200).send({ status: 'accepted' });
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotent consumer behavior under replay scenarios?
- What metrics and alarms would you configure to detect dedupe or latency anomalies?
